    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d216">

                <pb facs="0309" n="287" sameAs="frus:frus1951v02/pg287"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">310.265/1–1551</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Secretary of
                        State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d216-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1951-01-15">January 15, 1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Admission of Italy into the United Nations</p>
                <table>
                    <row role="label">
                        <cell rendition="#top #nowrap">Participants:</cell>
                        <cell>Ambassador Tarchiani, Italian Embassy</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>The Secretary of State</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Homer M. Byington, Jr., WE
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>Ambassador Tarchiani said that one impression he had received very strongly
                    during his visit to Italy was the concern of public opinion that Italy was
                    tending to be pushed on the sidelines when main issues were discussed. He felt
                    that the Italian people were very much aware of Italy’s failure to obtain
                    admission into the United Nations and that there was some impression that
                    Italy’s allies had not supported her strongly enough in that connection. He said
                    that if Communist China were to be admitted into the UN and Italy not, the resultant reaction on Italian public opinion
                    would be most unfavorable.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d216-f2"/> I said that as he
                    knew, we were strongly in favor of Italian admission into the UN.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d216-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d216-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted by the Director of the Office of Western European Affairs (Byington).
                    Initialed for the Secretary of State by Francis E. Meloy, Jr., of the Executive
                    Secretariat.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d216-f2" n="2" xml:id="d216-f2-content">
                <p>For documentation regarding the question of Chinese representation, see <ref
                        target="#pg209">pp. 209</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
